# Condition Variables

## What is a Condition Variable?

A condition variable allows a thread to **sleep until some condition becomes true**.

Instead of constantly checking:

```c
while (queue_empty()) {
    // keep checking
}
```

a thread can sleep:

```text
Thread
  ↓
Condition is false
  ↓
WAIT
  ↓
Another thread changes state
  ↓
SIGNAL
  ↓
Thread wakes up
  ↓
Check condition again
```

## Why Do We Need Them?

Suppose a consumer needs an item from a queue.

If the queue is empty, there is nothing useful for the consumer to do.

A condition variable lets it sleep until a producer adds something.

```text
Producer
   │
   ▼
 Queue
   │
   ▼
Consumer
```

## POSIX API

```c
pthread_cond_init()
pthread_cond_wait()
pthread_cond_signal()
pthread_cond_broadcast()
pthread_cond_destroy()
```

## Basic Pattern

```c
pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

while (!condition) {
    pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mutex);
}

pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
```

The condition variable is normally paired with a mutex.

## Why `while` Instead of `if`?

Always re-check the condition:

```c
while (!condition) {
    pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mutex);
}
```

A thread can wake up even though the condition is no longer true.

Therefore:

```text
Wake up
   ↓
Acquire mutex
   ↓
Check condition
   ↓
Still false? → sleep again
```

## Signal vs Broadcast

### `pthread_cond_signal()`

Wakes one waiting thread.

### `pthread_cond_broadcast()`

Wakes all waiting threads.

## Mental Model

A mutex protects the shared state.

A condition variable tells threads:

> "You can sleep until something changes."

## Practice

Implement a program where:

* One thread produces numbers.
* Another thread consumes them.
* The consumer waits when there is no number available.

